\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Full model versus matched no-strain baseline at $\nu =10^{-5}$ (ensemble means, $N=100$, $\pm \sigma $ band; centroid curves lightly smoothed). (\textit  {a}) Final-strain spectrum $E(k)=\DOTSI \sumop \slimits@ _i E_i(k)$: the full model (strain on) extends to higher wavenumber than the baseline (strain off) but remains broadband, not a rigid translation. (\textit  {b}) Centroid migration: the baseline relaxes below unity, the full model is lifted above it by the compression, yet stays far below the rigid-translation line (dashed). The gap between the two solid curves is the distortion attributable to the mean strain at this Reynolds number.}}{22}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spec-bvc}{{6}{22}{Full model versus matched no-strain baseline at $\nu =10^{-5}$ (ensemble means, $N=100$, $\pm \sigma $ band; centroid curves lightly smoothed). (\textit {a}) Final-strain spectrum $E(k)=\sum _i E_i(k)$: the full model (strain on) extends to higher wavenumber than the baseline (strain off) but remains broadband, not a rigid translation. (\textit {b}) Centroid migration: the baseline relaxes below unity, the full model is lifted above it by the compression, yet stays far below the rigid-translation line (dashed). The gap between the two solid curves is the distortion attributable to the mean strain at this Reynolds number}{figure.caption.7}{}}
